{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787774741,"nonce":"BykEmwqPEICIR3D6Rq9izO+YptA=","title":"chore(build): stop target/ growing without bound (100 GB → 4.6 GB) + finish the ACME de-identification","message":"\u003e Imported from pull request #86 (MERGED)\n\u003e Branch: `chore/build-disk-hygiene` → `main`\n\u003e Created: 2026-07-26T04:10:34Z · Merged: 2026-07-26T04:12:42Z\n\n`target/` in a two-week-old checkout reached **100 GB** — 66 GB of deps, 34 GB of incremental caches, **610,211 files** in `target/debug/deps` of which **88,331** were stale `scarab_server-*` variants. Across ~19 git worktrees that came to ~92 GB more.\n\nThe cause is not one setting: **cargo never garbage-collects `target/`**. Every fingerprint change — branch switch, dep bump, feature flag, rustflag — writes a new artifact and keeps the old one forever. Full DWARF then made each of those artifacts as large as it could be.\n\nNotably, CI already ran lean (`CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG: 0`, `CARGO_INCREMENTAL: 0`); only local builds didn't. This mostly closes that gap.\n\n\n**`just sweep [days]`** (default 7) — prunes artifacts untouched for N days, `--recursive` so sibling worktrees are covered too. This is the only change here that *bounds growth over time* rather than lowering the starting point; the other two reduce the footprint of a single build. `just disk` prints the breakdown it exists to fix. Requires `cargo install cargo-sweep`, and says so if it is missing.\n\n**`[profile.dev] debug = \"line-tables-only\"`** — keeps what this codebase actually reads (file and line in panics, backtraces, nextest failure output) and drops the variable-level DWARF it never uses; debugging here goes through test output, not lldb. **CI behaviour is unchanged**: it sets `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0` and the env var wins over the profile. One-off override for an actual debugger session: `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=2 cargo test -p \u003ccrate\u003e`.\n\n**`.cargo/config.toml` → `incremental = false`** — that was the entire 34 GB. Incremental buys least in exactly this repo's loop, where `just check` and `just test` compile the whole workspace, and it accumulates per fingerprint without bound. CI already set `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`; this makes local match. Override for a tight single-crate edit loop: `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=1 cargo check -p \u003ccrate\u003e`.\n\n\nAfter `cargo clean` plus a full `just test` rebuild under the new settings:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| `target/` | 100 GB | **4.6 GB** |\n| `target/debug/incremental` | 34 GB | **0 B** |\n| tests | — | 648/648 passing |\n\nHonest caveat: most of the 100 GB was two weeks of *accumulation*, not one build's footprint, and I did not measure a fresh build under the old settings — so this is not a clean \"the settings saved 95%\" claim. What it does show is that 4.6 GB is the real steady state now, and that the 34 GB incremental term is structurally gone rather than merely cleaned.\n\n\n**`.envrc`** (direnv) exporting `SCARAB_TEST_DATABASE_URL`. `just test` exports it already, but a test launched from an IDE/editor gutter never goes through the recipe — it inherits the shell, and without the URL the Postgres-backed tests skip or fail for a reason that looks like a code bug. See `docs/audits/2026-07-24-test-strategy.md`, which specifies this convention.\n\nIt deliberately does **not** `dotenv .env.local`, tempting as that one-liner is. That file holds durable server config *and* real secrets (OAuth client secret, master key), and this repo has already been bitten by precisely that failure: `deploy/local-helm/deploy.sh` carries a hardening block whose comment names it — *\"a stale ambient value — e.g. a direnv-loaded `.env.local` — must NOT silently override the in-cluster config\"* — and the dogfood handoff records the rule as *\"never let a stale direnv `.env.local` var win.\"* So exactly one variable becomes ambient, and `.env.local` stays a file that consumers load deliberately.\n\n**`.gitignore`**: `.cargo/config.toml` is tracked on purpose — repo-level build policy belongs in version control. `cargo login` writes a registry token beside it, so `.cargo/credentials{,.toml}` is now ignored. That file does not exist yet, which is exactly why guarding it is free today.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---\n\n\nA second, unrelated commit is folded into this PR by request rather than raised separately.\n\nIt replaces the last **15 occurrences of the real employer name with ACME** across ADR-0055, four handoff docs, and the local-helm context guard — finishing the neutral-placeholder scrub that `a927558` began for screenshots (`org=acme`). This commit had been sitting unpushed on a local branch, so **`main` still carried the name in all 6 files** until now; `git grep` on this branch returns no occurrences.\n\nThe one non-docs file is a comment in `deploy/local-helm/deploy.sh`, now reading \"never an ACME EKS ctx\". The guard it documents is a positive allowlist (`ctx = colima`), so the rename is cosmetic and cannot weaken it. `bash -n` passes.","files":null}]}